---
title: Runlevels
order: 2
description: rc-update add/del/show, default/boot/shutdown runlevels
tags: [openrc, runlevels, rc-update, boot, default]
---

Runlevels define which services start at which stage. rc-update manages service-to-runlevel assignments.

@text
Viewing Runlevels

@code bash
# Show all services and their runlevels
rc-update show

# Show services in a specific runlevel
rc-update show default
rc-update show boot
rc-update show shutdown

# Show verbose (includes inactive services)
rc-update -v show

@text
Managing Service Runlevels

@code bash
# Add a service to the default runlevel (starts at normal boot)
sudo rc-update add sshd default

# Add to the boot runlevel (starts early, before default)
sudo rc-update add hwclock boot

# Add to shutdown runlevel
sudo rc-update add savecache shutdown

# Remove a service from a runlevel
sudo rc-update del sshd default

# Move a service between runlevels
sudo rc-update del sshd default
sudo rc-update add sshd boot

@text
Standard Runlevels

@code bash
# sysinit  — earliest stage, mounts filesystems, sets hostname
#            (managed by OpenRC itself, usually don't add services here)

# boot     — essential services needed before user login
#            hwclock, modules, sysctl, hostname, keymaps, udev, etc.
#            Runs after sysinit.

# default  — normal operation services
#            NetworkManager, sshd, cronie, display manager, etc.
#            Runs after boot. This is where most services go.

# shutdown — cleanup before power off
#            savecache, killprocs, mount-ro
#            Runs on shutdown/reboot.

# View contents of each runlevel directory
ls /etc/runlevels/default/
ls /etc/runlevels/boot/
ls /etc/runlevels/shutdown/

# These directories contain symlinks to init scripts
ls -la /etc/runlevels/default/

@text
Custom Runlevels

@code bash
# Create a custom runlevel
sudo mkdir /etc/runlevels/minimal

# Add services to the custom runlevel
sudo rc-update add udev minimal
sudo rc-update add dbus minimal
sudo rc-update add elogind minimal
sudo rc-update add sshd minimal

# Boot into a custom runlevel (add to kernel params)
# In GRUB: softlevel=minimal

# Switch runlevel at runtime
sudo openrc minimal

# Switch back to default
sudo openrc default

@text
Practical Examples

@code bash
# Typical default runlevel services on an Artix desktop:
sudo rc-update add elogind default
sudo rc-update add NetworkManager default
sudo rc-update add sddm default
sudo rc-update add dbus default
sudo rc-update add cronie default
sudo rc-update add syslog-ng default
sudo rc-update add bluetoothd default

# Typical boot runlevel:
sudo rc-update add udev boot
sudo rc-update add hwclock boot
sudo rc-update add keymaps boot
sudo rc-update add modules boot
sudo rc-update add sysctl boot
